WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.753 --> 00:00:25.418
Hola, amigos! That's Spanish for "Hello, friends!" I'm learning Spanish because—get this—my parents might let me study abroad in Spain this summer!

2
00:00:25.419 --> 00:00:34.507
And it's all thanks to Mr. Fay. He told us about a landmark there that sounds so cool, I want to check it out.

3
00:00:34.508 --> 00:00:40.438
It's a medieval fortress and complex of palaces called the Alhambra.

4
00:00:40.462 --> 00:00:45.011
The word alhambra means "the red" in Arabic.

5
00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:53.400
The complex was named that because the entire place is surrounded by walls made of red gravel and clay bricks.

6
00:00:53.401 --> 00:01:03.330
From the outside the Alhambra looks pretty plain, but on the inside, everything is decorated with fancy tiles and carvings.

7
00:01:03.331 --> 00:01:15.223
Oh, and here's a cool fact: the walls are covered with calligraphy because the original builders were actually ordered to write all over them!

8
00:01:15.224 --> 00:01:25.132
So why does a place in Spain have an Arabic name? Mr. Fay had to give us a little history first so we could understand that.

9
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:32.275
In the 700s Arab Muslims invaded and took over large parts of Spain.

10
00:01:32.276 --> 00:01:41.443
For the next 700 years or so, Spain was a battleground for Christians and these Muslims, known as Moors.

11
00:01:41.444 --> 00:01:50.127
Eventually, in 1492, Catholic monarchs regained control and ousted the Moors from their kingdom.

12
00:01:50.128 --> 00:01:58.841
But the Moors left their mark with the Alhambra, a beautiful walled city they'd built in Granada.

13
00:01:58.842 --> 00:02:08.591
Years before, when the Moors were fighting Christian armies, the rulers had needed a military fortress that would be naturally protected.

14
00:02:08.592 --> 00:02:15.367
On a rocky hill in southern Spain, they found an old fort from the eighth century.

15
00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:23.090
The hill was near a river, so there was access to water, and the area was protected by mountains and woods.

16
00:02:23.091 --> 00:02:28.992
It was the ideal place to put their lookout tower and royal residence.

17
00:02:28.993 --> 00:02:40.322
Over time Moorish rulers continued to expand the Alhambra; construction began during the ninth century and continued through the late fifteenth century.

18
00:02:40.323 --> 00:02:51.588
At its peak the complex contained a military fortress, two palaces and courts, artisan workshops, baths, and a mosque.

19
00:02:51.589 --> 00:02:58.492
The oldest remaining section of the complex is the military area, known as the Alcazaba.

20
00:02:58.493 --> 00:03:05.738
It contains several towers, including the original watchtower, which is about 27 meters tall.

21
00:03:05.739 --> 00:03:15.201
After climbing the steep, narrow staircase to the top, royal guards could watch for invaders and even attack enemies from above.

22
00:03:15.202 --> 00:03:19.015
I bet it felt like the top of the world up there!

23
00:03:19.039 --> 00:03:28.113
The Alcazaba also had military barracks, secret passageways, and even underground dungeons for prisoners!

24
00:03:28.113 --> 00:03:34.011
Another section of the Alhambra is a great hall known as the Mexuar.

25
00:03:34.035 --> 00:03:38.465
Remember how I said there was lots of writing on the walls?

26
00:03:38.465 --> 00:03:51.342
Well, Islamic law forbids the drawing or painting of living things, so all the decorations in the Alhambra had to be geometric designs or calligraphy.

27
00:03:51.343 --> 00:03:56.166
I think it was a really smart idea to write on the walls.

28
00:03:56.167 --> 00:04:02.847
For example, at the door to the Mexuar, it says, "Enter and ask.

29
00:04:02.871 --> 00:04:08.337
Do not be afraid to seek justice for here you will find it."

30
00:04:08.337 --> 00:04:18.434
And guess what went on there? That's right—official government business, including all matters of justice and the law.

31
00:04:18.435 --> 00:04:25.991
It was where the sultan and government officials held meetings and public forums for people to voice complaints.

32
00:04:25.992 --> 00:04:30.972
The fanciest parts of the Alhambra are the palaces, of course.

33
00:04:30.973 --> 00:04:38.621
Two were built during the Arab rule, and a third was commissioned later by a Christian king in the 1500s.

34
00:04:38.622 --> 00:04:44.986
The two Arab palaces are the Comares Palace and the Palace of the Lions.

35
00:04:44.987 --> 00:04:53.186
The Comares Palace was the official royal residence, built between 1333 and 1370.

36
00:04:53.187 --> 00:04:57.085
The sultan lived there with his wives and guards.

37
00:04:57.109 --> 00:05:02.951
From the pictures Mr. Fay showed us, it sure looks like a sultan's home!

38
00:05:02.951 --> 00:05:09.774
The palace is centered around a courtyard that has a long pool fed by two fountains.

39
00:05:09.775 --> 00:05:16.432
Green myrtle bushes line the pool, giving the courtyard its name—the Court of Myrtle.

40
00:05:16.433 --> 00:05:24.534
In the right light, the water reflects the bushes' color, making the edges of the pool look green.

41
00:05:24.535 --> 00:05:31.574
The courtyard also has a white marble patio with intricately decorated arches and columns.

42
00:05:31.575 --> 00:05:38.844
Historians think the Alhambra was built primarily to show the greatness of Allah, or God.

43
00:05:38.845 --> 00:05:46.661
Every sight within the complex was supposed to remind people of the beauty of Allah's creation.

44
00:05:46.662 --> 00:05:55.259
The Palace of the Lions, another royal residence, was built between 1362 and 1391.

45
00:05:55.260 --> 00:06:05.325
Its courtyard has a magnificent fountain supported by twelve lions spouting water—the only representations of living forms in the Alhambra.

46
00:06:05.326 --> 00:06:15.486
The courtyard is supported by white marble columns, and its ceilings are decorated with carved, lace-like designs, and fancy calligraphy.

47
00:06:15.487 --> 00:06:21.128
I can't even imagine how long it must have taken to carve all that by hand!

48
00:06:21.129 --> 00:06:33.115
In 1527 the Christian king, Charles V, decided that he liked the Alhambra so much, he wanted to build himself a summer palace there.

49
00:06:33.116 --> 00:06:40.760
He planned for it to be bigger and better than the Arab palaces, and built in a Christian style.

50
00:06:40.761 --> 00:06:53.082
But unfortunately for Charles V, the construction wasn't completed until 1957 because he ran out of money. Oops!

51
00:06:53.083 --> 00:07:02.656
Today the Alhambra is the only remaining palace complex that can show us the beauty of medieval Muslim design and architecture.

52
00:07:02.657 --> 00:07:09.084
Sadly it no longer looks exactly like it did during the Middle Ages and the Renaissance.

53
00:07:09.085 --> 00:07:20.746
After 1492 Christian monarchs regained control of Spain and destroyed sections of the Alhambra, rebuilding them in European Renaissance style.

54
00:07:20.747 --> 00:07:29.731
The Alhambra has since faced fires, earthquakes, gunpowder explosions, and wreckage caused by tourists.

55
00:07:29.732 --> 00:07:40.163
As recently as fifty years ago, visitors were carving their names into the walls and chipping off pieces of tiles as souvenirs!

56
00:07:40.164 --> 00:07:49.792
Luckily in 1984 the Alhambra was named a World Heritage site, so it's now strictly preserved.

57
00:07:49.793 --> 00:08:00.971
Well, I'd better keep practicing my Spanish. My parents said I can only go to Spain if I speak nothing but Spanish to them until June.

58
00:08:00.972 --> 00:08:09.545
And a trip filled with medieval castles sounds too fun to pass up! See you next time!
